Got it. For a whole-codebase manual review, I’d read in compiler-flow order, then circle back to runtime/stdlib. That gives you the invariants before the implementation details.
Recommended Order
1. .cursor/rules/*, README.md, Justfile, CMakeLists.txt
   Establish contracts, build/test entrypoints, and intended boundaries.
2. include/noria/CompileOptions.hpp, src/main.cpp, include/noria/Compiler.hpp, src/Compiler.cpp
   CLI and top-level pipeline orchestration.
3. include/noria/Token.hpp, include/noria/Lexer.hpp, src/Lexer.cpp
   Lexical syntax, source locations, literal parsing edges.
4. include/noria/Ast*.hpp, src/AstPrinter.cpp, include/noria/Parser.hpp, src/Parser.cpp
   Language surface, AST ownership, parser diagnostics.
5. include/noria/Type.hpp, src/Type.cpp
   Type identity and formatting. This is a core invariant file.
6. include/noria/ModuleResolver.hpp, src/ModuleResolver.cpp
   Imports, visibility, module ownership, duplicate exports, private runtime handling.
7. include/noria/TypeChecker.hpp, src/TypeChecker.cpp
   Biggest semantic review area: scope rules, inference, literal bounds, generics, visibility, runtime builtins.
8. include/noria/Monomorphize.hpp, src/Monomorphize.cpp
   Generic function/struct specialization, deterministic rewrites, cycles/reuse.
9. include/noria/Codegen.hpp, src/Codegen.cpp, include/noria/IrEmitter.hpp, src/IrEmitter.cpp
   LLVM text generation, traps, allocation, indexing, strings, aggregate layout.
10. include/noria/Optimizer.hpp, src/Optimizer.cpp, include/noria/NativeLinker.hpp, src/NativeLinker.cpp
       External tooling boundaries and failure behavior.
11. stdlib/**/*.noria
       Review internal runtime first, then public containers:
       stdlib/internal/rt.noria, memory/hash/layout helpers, then sequence/dictionary/set/heap APIs.
12. tests/*, tests/run_examples.sh, examples/basic/*, diagnostics fixtures
       End by mapping coverage to the invariants you just reviewed.
Highest-Priority Areas
If you want a bug-hunting shortlist: TypeChecker.cpp, Monomorphize.cpp, Codegen.cpp, ModuleResolver.cpp, and stdlib/internal/* are where I’d spend the most time. The highest-risk themes are generic specialization correctness, private/public visibility, type equality/stringification drift, array/string bounds traps, raw memory layout in stdlib containers, and diagnostics staying stable across imported files.